WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.049
Netflix's latest horror hit is thrilling viewers
even as it challenges the audience to figure

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.100
out what the heck is actually happening.

00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:12.340
From blades to bugs and that wild final act,
here's our breakdown of what it all means

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.980
in the ending of The Perfection.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.240
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.890
The Perfection's story is told in fragments
presented out of order.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:24.320
That first becomes clear when we see Charlotte
and Lizzie's trip through China first through

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.720
Lizzie's eyes, drugged and without context,
and then from Charlotte's perspective.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.700
Telling the story this way was a risky move
for director Richard Shepard and company,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.630
but non-traditional story structure has paid
off in the past for movies like Reservoir

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.430
Dogs and Eternal Sunshine of the Spotless
Mind films for which planting uncertainty

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.460
in the audience leads to a satisfying payoff
and makes trying to guess what's going on

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.140
part of the fun.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.500
The Perfection adds to this by attaching that
uncertainty to what should be a pretty simple

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.000
question in a horror picture: who's the monster
here?

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:00.040
It's only as the story nears its violent climax
that we truly understand that the real monster

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.520
is Anton, head of the Bachoff Academy.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.330
The events of The Perfection may feature a
ton of twists and narrative shifts, but the

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.600
action and horror is all part of the journey
to the end.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.160
A decade after leaving Bachoff Academy, Charlotte
is still trying to deal with the damage done

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.080
to her at the school.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.550
From the start, we get the impression that
this woman has unresolved, possibly dangerous

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.560
mental issues.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.540
The tension mounts as she heads to Shanghai
to meet her former teacher, Anton.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.890
There, Charlotte falls back into the good
graces of the school's staff and gets seduced

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.520
by the new star pupil, Lizzie.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.810
The two decide to take a trip into the Chinese
countryside together, and stuff starts to

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:40.680
get prescription strength weird.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.440
On the trip, Charlotte drugs Lizzie with medication
she took from her mother, inducing a stomach

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.120
flu and hallucinations.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.480
The two are kicked off their bus in the middle
of nowhere, and Charlotte convinces a delusional

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:57.060
Lizzie that her hand is full of bugs, prompting
Lizzie to go 127 Hours on herself with a cleaver.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:06.000
We learn towards the very end of the film
that after Lizzie wakes up, Charlotte is still

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:06.540
with her.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.340
She tells Lizzie that this was the only way
to free her from the Academy's brainwashing,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.740
and that they'll turn their backs on her now
that she's no longer useful to them.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.160
Charlotte returns home, and Lizzie tries to
go back to Bachoff.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.450
Although Anton lets Lizzie stay at Bachoff
for awhile, he eventually kicks her out just

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:24.640
like Charlotte predicted.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.560
Bitter and lost, Lizzie proceeds to break
into Charlotte's house, intent on killing

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:30.060
her.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.680
Then Lizzie brings Charlotte back to the Bachoff
Academy as a prisoner.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.380
She's chained up and forced to play for the
staff and misses one note.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.590
As the faculty close in on her, though, they
start falling to the floor because Lizzie

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:42.860
has drugged their drinks.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.420
Through the final flashback, we return to
Charlotte's house and see Lizzie finally acknowledging

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.599
that Charlotte was right she'd been brainwashed,
and they both agree that the creeps at Bachoff

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.660
need to be stopped.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.819
In the final shots of the movie, we see Charlotte
and Lizzie playing a single cello together,

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:58.820
each with one arm.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:04.000
Anton, meanwhile, is dismembered, blinded,
and castrated, hooked up to a feeding tube,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.160
being forced to listen.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.160
It's a bizarre, yet emotional moment for both
the audience and for Lizzie and Charlotte.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.900
The room they're in, the Chapel, had always
been a sacred place for Anton.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.320
It's where he dreamed of hearing perfection.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:20.620
Veteran students were brought in there to
play a perfect piece, only to be punished

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:21.840
when they missed a note.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.980
"We don't make mistakes here, not when we
represent the academy."

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.980
With all his limbs gone and his eyes and mouth
sewn shut, Anton is essentially locked in

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.680
a mental prison where he's forced to listen
to Charlotte and Lizzie play forever.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.020
What if they miss a note?

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:39.020
Who cares!

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.840
They're playing for themselves now, and Anton
has no choice but to endure it.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:48.140
It probably goes without saying that this
movie is rooted deeply in recent social phenomena

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.560
like the Time's Up and #MeToo movements, and
it brings a level of fictionalized catharsis

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.540
in the form of a gruesome conclusion.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.480
This is a long-running tradition in the horror
genre: a character or force representing a

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.740
real-world fear is brought down by the forces
of good.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:08.440
Alien famously used its xenomorphs as an allegory
for sexual violence, and Get Out pretty brilliantly

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.300
turned racism and gentrification into a Frankenstein
monstrosity.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.580
In the same way, The Perfection tackles institutionalized
abuse, gaslighting, and toxic misuse of power.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:22.940
When Charlotte and Lizzie force a severely
de-limbed Anton to listen to them play, they're

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.960
not just reclaiming their power, they're asserting
their self-worth over an abusive power structure

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.960
that spent years objectifying them.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.500
If you saw the trailer for The Perfection,
you were probably pretty intrigued.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.560
You were also probably wildly thrown off when
you watched the actual movie.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:43.370
The trailer for The Perfection made it out
to be something it was decidedly not: a bug

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.140
infestation freakout picture.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:49.260
It made heavy use of the scene when, stranded
on the side of the road, Lizzie screams at

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.200
Charlotte about the bugs crawling inside of
her arm.

00:04:52.729 --> 00:04:56.700
Beyond that, we're not given much information
as to what the film is about, besides being

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:00.499
informed that Charlotte is the kind of person
who's always prepared in case a meat cleaver

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.160
might come in, wait for it, handy.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.360
"You know what you have to do."

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.580
Netflix was pretty cautious about not giving
anything away in the trailer, and it makes

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:10.080
sense.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.380
The movie's emotional gravity is predicated
on the audience having as little information

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:15.120
as possible.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:19.240
And in a messed up sort of way, it's kind
of fitting that a movie based on surprising

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.840
twists was marketed through yet another twist.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.920
With everything happening onscreen, the audience
could pretty easily overlook some of the musical

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.160
cues in The Perfection.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.500
The thing is, some of that music is pretty
important.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:36.080
For one thing, Anton is pretty consistently
associated with Mozart's Requiem in D minor.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.530
It's a bombastic, foreboding series of pieces,
the most dramatic of which, "Dies Irae," plays

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:46.439
when Anton approaches Lizzie in the driveway,
unaware that he's about to be horribly dismembered

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.260
as a result of his own misdeeds.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.779
Aside from the song's lyrics about judgement
and reckoning, you could go even deeper with

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:53.840
this choice of music.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.520
The story goes that Mozart told his wife that
he was writing the mass for his own funeral.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.520
When he died before completing the Requiem,
he left the completion of the composition

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.080
to one of his former assistants.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:08.399
Intentional or not, it's a fitting song to
play as Anton arrives at his eventual doom

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.900
in a school which will soon be controlled
by his own former students.

